# Airflow's plugin loader recursively execs every .py file under this
# folder and registers each one in sys.modules keyed by its bare filename
# stem (no package prefix), just to check whether it defines an
# AirflowPlugin subclass. A file that happens to share a stdlib module's
# name -- e.g. any of this project's email.py files -- clobbers
# sys.modules[<stem>], breaking `import email` (and therefore smtplib,
# and therefore airflow.utils.email) for every plugin file scanned
# afterward, in whichever process ran the scan. Verified against Airflow
# 3.3.0: this is not one file's bug, it's the scan mechanism itself.
#
# Only main.py files register AirflowPlugin subclasses in this project;
# everything else is plain implementation code imported via normal dotted
# imports elsewhere and was never meant to be exec'd by this scanner.
# Restricting the scan to main.py avoids the collision entirely.
*.py
!main.py
